%[text] # Assignment 4 — Working with Struct Data
%[text] Your ship's radar has detected three surface contacts. The contact data is stored in `contactdata.mat`.
%[text] Work through each part to inspect, report on, and update the contact picture.
%%
%[text] ## Part 1 — Load and Inspect
%[text] Load the contact data and inspect the `contact` variable in the **Workspace** panel.
%[text] Notice it is a **1×3 struct array** — three contacts, each with the same fields.
clear;
load contactdata.mat
%[text] Access individual fields from a single contact using dot notation.
contact(1).id
contact(1).bearing
contact(1).range
%%
%[text] ## Part 2 — Display All Contacts
%[text] Use `fprintf` to print a formatted contact report for all three contacts.
%[text] Index into the struct array with `contact(k)` to step through each element.  The resulting formatted output should look like this...
%[text] ![](text:image:3d51)
% Your code here
%%
%[text] ## Part 3 — Update a Contact
%[text] A new radar sweep shows Contact C-02 has maneuvered — bearing and range have changed.
%[text] Update only the fields that changed. The rest of the record stays intact.
%[text] - Change the `bearing` of `C-02` to a value of `120`.
%[text] - Change the `range` of `C-02` to a value of `6.8`.  \
% Your code here
%[text] Reprint the report to confirm the update.
% Your code here
%%
%[text] ## Part 4 — Modify a Field
%[text] The `range` field is currently in nautical miles, but we need the report in yards (1 nm = 2025 yards).
%[text] Update the `range` field for each contact in the struct array.
% Your code here
%%
%[text] ## Part 5 — Add a New Field
%[text] After further observation you can now classify each contact.
%[text] Add a `classification` field — MATLAB allows new fields to be added to a struct at any time.
%[text] For each contact, add a `classification` field with the following string values:
%[text] 1. `"MERCHANT"`
%[text] 2. `"UNKNOWN"`
%[text] 3. `"WARSHIP"` \
